{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Trained non-expert panels using explicit checklists outperform single experts on verifiable tasks only when the checklist encodes the diagnostic cues the expert would use implicitly; the reliability gain is mechanistic, driven by preventing cue-omission under cognitive load.","counterconditions":["If the checklist omits a cue the expert uses tacitly (e.g. configural cue interaction), panel mean accuracy can fall below expert accuracy.","If the task is perceptual rather than propositional (e.g. radiology, wine tasting), checklist compliance may degrade signal by forcing verbal proxies.","If non-experts are untrained on cue definitions, checklist items are interpreted idiosyncratically, inflating variance."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition operates as a fast pattern-matcher that typically recapitulates a cue-list, but under noise, fatigue, or base-rate pressures it omits cues. The checklist forces sequential, accountable cue sampling; aggregating across non-experts then cancels individual cue-sampling errors, yielding lower variance than a single expert whose cue omission is systematic within that one trial.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Checklist-driven non-expert panels do not gain genuine reliability on verifiable tasks; they produce high inter-rater agreement that is mistaken for accuracy because the checklist anchors reasoning on a shared, plausible-but-wrong frame.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is empirically calibrated against outcomes (actuarial model), agreement and accuracy co-vary and the panel genuinely outperforms experts.","If panelists' interpretations of checklist items are independent conditional on the truth, averaging cancels bias and the anchor objection fails."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklists reduce variance by anchoring all panelists to the same operationalization; this raises agreement without raising accuracy if the checklist encodes a wrong model. The appearance of reliability is a compositional artifact of shared anchoring, not evidence of truth-tracking.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The key distinguisher between genuine reliability gain and mere checklist compliance is out-of-distribution transfer: a genuine gain persists when the checklist is applied to a rotated item set with shifted base rates, while mere compliance collapses because it depends on surface-feature matching to the checklist's calibration set.","counterconditions":["If the domain is narrow and the OOD set is too far from the training distribution, even a genuine cue-abstraction strategy may fail, collapsing the distinguisher.","If the checklist is extremely fine-grained and exhaustive, feature-matching may be indistinguishable from cue-abstraction within any practical evaluation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Genuine reliability is cue-abstraction: panelists learn which checklist items carry diagnostic weight and apply them to novel instances. Mere compliance is feature-matching to the exact checklist phrasing; when surface features shift, the matching fails. Within a single evaluation, OOD item rotation (with unchanged latent structure) isolates cue-abstraction from rote compliance.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A single evaluation cannot distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance on its own; the distinction is fundamentally longitudinal and mechanistic, requiring comparison across items where the checklist is misaligned with the latent structure of the domain.","counterconditions":["If the single evaluation includes a held-out adversarial item subset where checklist-cues and outcome-cues diverge, it can in fact separate compliance from genuine reliability, contradicting the claim.","If the domain is such that checklist and outcome structure are logically identical, the distinction is vacuous and a single evaluation suffices."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Mere compliance tracks the checklist; genuine reliability tracks the latent outcome structure. Only when the checklist is deliberately perturbed (misaligned with outcome) can one observe whether panel performance follows the checklist (compliance) or the outcome (genuine). A single unperturbed evaluation cannot separate these because the checklist and outcome are aligned by construction.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's advantage is not from the checklist but from aggregation: a panel of trained non-experts without a checklist still outperforms a single expert on verifiable tasks because independent errors cancel, and the checklist merely standardizes cue access to reduce correlated errors.","counterconditions":["If errors are correlated (e.g. all panelists share a misconception), aggregation does not help and the checklist can worsen correlation.","If the expert's accuracy is very high (>0.9) and the panelists are barely above chance, a small panel may not outperform the expert.","If the task has a strong configural component, independent cue-sampling may produce systematic majority errors."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"By the Condorcet jury theorem, if each panelist has accuracy > 0.5 and errors are independent, the majority accuracy approaches 1 as panel size grows. The expert is a single draw with no cancellation. The checklist's causal role is secondary: it reduces correlated error by ensuring all panelists sample the same cues, thereby making the independence assumption more likely to hold.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.68},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"On verifiable tasks, the panel-plus-checklist will reliably outperform the single expert only when the expert's intuition is subject to known biases the checklist explicitly counters; otherwise, the expert's tacit configural knowledge dominates and the panel's additive cue model underperforms.","counterconditions":["If the expert is unbiased and the task is genuinely configural, the additive panel model fails to capture interactions and underperforms.","If the checklist includes interaction terms, it approximates a configural model and the additive/configural distinction collapses.","If the expert's bias is unknown, the bias-correction mechanism cannot be verified within a single evaluation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is a single integrated readout of a configural model learned via extensive exposure; the checklist forces an additive (cue-by-cue) model. Additive models outperform configural models only when the configural model is distorted by bias (e.g. base-rate neglect). The causal mechanism is bias-correction, not aggregation per se. The distinguishing evidence within one evaluation is whether panel advantage concentrates on items where the expert's known bias applies.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}